**Name the design.** Two valid answers, and it helps to say both and choose one.

1. **Template Method** (inheritance): a base class defines the fixed algorithm `makeBeverage()` that calls abstract steps in order; subclasses override the steps. Simple, but the sequence is fixed at compile time and you get a subclass per drink.
2. **Strategy / Command pipeline with composition** (preferred here): each step is an object behind a common interface, and a recipe is an ordered list of steps. Swapping sugar for coffee means replacing one list element, without touching any other class. This follows the open/closed principle and the dependency inversion principle.

**Code sketch.**
```java
interface Step { void execute(Machine m); }

class PourMilk implements Step { public void execute(Machine m) { m.dispense("milk", 50); } }
class AddSugar implements Step { public void execute(Machine m) { m.dispense("sugar", 5); } }
class AddWater implements Step { public void execute(Machine m) { m.dispense("water", 100); } }

class Recipe {
    private final String name;
    private final List<Step> steps;
    Recipe(String name, List<Step> steps) { this.name = name; this.steps = List.copyOf(steps); }
    void run(Machine m) { for (Step s : steps) s.execute(m); }
}

class Machine {
    private final Map<String,Integer> stock = new HashMap<>();
    void dispense(String item, int qty) {
        if (stock.getOrDefault(item, 0) < qty) throw new OutOfStockException(item);
        stock.merge(item, -qty, Integer::sum);
    }
    void make(Recipe r) { r.run(this); }
}
```
A new drink is a new `Recipe` built from existing steps; replacing sugar is `new Recipe("latte", List.of(new PourMilk(), new AddCoffee(), new AddWater()))`. A `RecipeFactory` or builder keeps recipe definitions in one place; reading recipes from configuration makes them changeable without code changes.

**Extensions to cover.** Check ingredient availability before starting (validate the whole recipe first so a partial drink is never made), a state machine for the machine (idle, selecting, brewing, error), payment as another strategy, and thread-safety if requests can overlap (one brew at a time via a lock).

**Why it is good:** steps are independent, individually testable and reusable across recipes.
